.TH DOSE 1 "2026-07-30" "bashified" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
dose \- apportion this ecosystem's scheduled work
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dose
[\fIlist\fR]
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B dose
\fIproject\fR [\fB\-\-check\fR|\fB\-\-apply\fR]
.br
.B dose
\-\-help
.br
.B dose
\-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
A plain shell utility. It takes one argument, a project name, keeps a stated
promise about it, and exits with a code that says exactly what happened.
.PP
Run
.B dose list
for the one form this verb has. Earlier builds carried a 25-entry
subcommand table, one arm per script discovered in the legacy tree; cut
entire in hf7y/scheduler#296 after zero of the 25 was ever invoked as
.B "dose <sub>" .
.SH THE PROJECT FORM
.B dose \fIproject\fR
names a
.B project
in
.IR schedule/ROSTER ,
and makes this host match the row it finds there: the account exists, its
clone is present and clean, and its crontab carries the paced-runner line at
the rate the roster declares, staggered by name.
.PP
The roster is read from
.B GitHub
on every run, never from a local clone. A clone is whatever that host last
happened to pull, and the host you type this on is the one most likely to be
stale.
.PP
.B \-\-check
is the default and writes nothing.
.B \-\-apply
converges, then re-reads the crontab and compares it against what the roster
asked for; a mismatch exits 5 rather than reporting success.
A row marked
.B parked
arms nothing under either flag. A row naming another host stops at exit 7
without acting. A project absent from the roster exits 4, never 0.
.PP
.B Only a human edits the roster.
This command converges TO it and never writes it. Anything that edits a row
and then runs this has armed itself, which is precisely what
.IR schedule/FREEZE
reserves for a person.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet, \-q
Results only, no commentary.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Report the difference between this host and the roster, and write nothing.
The default, and meaningful only in the project form described below.
.TP
.B \-\-apply
Converge, then verify by re-reading. Project form only.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Usage summary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH THE COST BOUNDARY
This utility does not spend money and therefore has
.B no --summon flag.
Any utility in this family that CAN spend declares
.B \-\-summon
and refuses to spend without it. There is deliberately no short form:
.B \-s
collides with existing tools and
.B \-S
differs from it by a
single shift key, which is an unacceptable property for the one flag
that costs real money. Spelling the word out is the deliberateness.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The promise was kept.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error; the caller is wrong.
.TP
.B 3
Needs a summon and did not get one. A finding, not an error.
.TP
.B 4
GAP: the tooling to keep this promise does not exist yet.
.TP
.B 5
BROKEN: it ran and produced a wrong or partial answer.
.TP
.B 6
BLIND: it cannot read its domain, so it reports nothing about it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the version and the code it exits with:
.PP
.nf
$ dose \-\-version
dose (bashified)
$ echo $?
0
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I CONTRACT.md
The promise this utility must keep.
.TP
.I GAPS.md
What the contract names and the tooling cannot yet do.
.SH HISTORY
Rebuilt 2026-07-30 from the tree named
.BR scheduler .
This branch is a total purge: it keeps the tool and nothing else.
What was removed is not lost \- it is on the default branch
of this same repository. That is why this is a branch and not a new
repository, and extracting it into one would destroy the archive that
justifies the purge.
.PP
Cut again 2026-08-29 (hf7y/scheduler#296): the 25-subcommand passthrough
table this tool launched with is gone. Measured 2026-08-28, none of the 25
had ever been invoked as
.BR "dose <sub>" .
The project form was never part of that table and is unchanged.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dose (1)
